#achievement(title: [DecodeME Brain Tissue Enrichment])[
*Certainty: 0.80.* DecodeME's MAGMA gene-tissue analysis revealed significant enrichment of ME/CFS-related genes in all 13 brain tissues examined, providing genetic evidence for central nervous system involvement in ME/CFS @DecodeME2025. Brain-tissue enrichment of this kind is not specific to ME/CFS and can arise for many polygenic traits @Finucane2018stratifiedLDSC; it indicates where ME/CFS-associated variants are statistically concentrated rather than establishing disease-specific CNS causality. Implicated genes include CA10 (CNS development, chronic pain), ARFGEF2 (vesicle trafficking, neuronal function), and UNC13C (glutamatergic synaptic transmission). The brain tissue enrichment finding is consistent with the neuroimaging abnormalities, neurochemical dysregulation, and HPA axis dysfunction documented in ME/CFS (Chapters @ch:neurological and @ch:endocrine).

This finding has been extended by the Maccallini 2026 meta-GWAS (n=19,470 cases), which incorporates DecodeME and is therefore partially overlapping rather than independent; it found brain and pituitary enrichment across 30 tissues with no other tissue reaching significance, and additionally performed cell-type enrichment identifying medium spiny neurons (MSNs) in the striatum as the most specific cell-type hit (MSN specificity is estimated at 0.40--0.50; the 0.80 figure applies to brain-*tissue*-level enrichment) @Maccallini2026metaGWAS. A stratified LDSC analysis using the Finucane et al.\ 2018 pipeline applied the same DecodeME GWAS to several reference datasets (GTEx v6, Dropviz mouse atlas, Human Brain Atlas)---an annotation-sensitivity check rather than an independent replication---and, against the *mouse* ImmGen reference, found no significant immune cell-type associations in a single unpublished, power-limited analysis (absence of evidence, not evidence of absence) @Finucane2018stratifiedLDSC. A further reference analysis using the DESCARTES fetal human atlas (same DecodeME GWAS) found neuronal rather than immune cell-type involvement @Lee2026geneticOverlap, and an additional same-analyst, unpublished line applied a chromatin-accessibility (snATAC-seq) atlas and found the same broad neuronal-versus-immune pattern @Li2023ATACbrain. See Achievement @ach:cell-type-enrichment-convergence in Chapter @ch:neurological for the cell-type specificity discussion.

Study: (n=15,579 cases, n=259,909 controls, MAGMA analysis, 13 brain tissues, partially reproduced in a partially overlapping meta-GWAS (incorporates DecodeME) n=19,470, convergent with 3 reference atlases (same GWAS) plus a same-analyst, unpublished chromatin sensitivity check; key caveat: fine cell-type resolution is method-dependent).

The MZ twin discordant study proposal in Chapter 47 (@sec:mz-twin-discordant-striatal-microbiome-lsr) provides a genetically controlled design to test whether this neuronal genetic signal translates to functional striatal pathology.
] <ach:decodeme-brain-enrichment>
